Meridian University Hostel and Disciplinary Regulations

Document ID: MU-RULEBOOK-2026-HOSTEL
Version: 2026.1
Effective Date: July 1, 2026
Issuing Authority: Office of Student Affairs

19. Hostel Regulations
19.1 Hostel Eligibility

Hostel accommodation may be provided to eligible students subject to availability of rooms and compliance with university accommodation requirements.

Priority may be given to students whose permanent residence is outside the local commuting area.

Admission to a university programme does not automatically guarantee hostel accommodation.

19.2 Room Allocation

Rooms are allocated by the university hostel administration.

Students must occupy only the room assigned to them unless a formal room-transfer request has been approved.

Students must not exchange rooms informally without authorization.

19.3 Hostel Registration

Students receiving hostel accommodation must complete the required hostel registration process before occupying a room.

Required registration information may include student identification details, emergency contact information, and other information required by hostel administration.

19.4 Residence Rules

Residents must comply with hostel operating hours, safety requirements, visitor procedures, and other published hostel instructions.

The hostel administration may issue additional reasonable instructions concerning safety, maintenance, and orderly operation.

19.5 Visitors

Visitors may enter hostel premises only in accordance with the applicable visitor policy.

A resident may be required to register a visitor with hostel security.

Visitors may be restricted from entering residential areas during designated quiet hours.

19.6 Quiet Hours

Quiet hours apply from 10:00 PM to 6:00 AM unless the hostel administration publishes a different schedule for a specific period.

During quiet hours, residents must avoid activities that materially disturb other residents.

19.7 Prohibited Activities

Residents must not:

damage hostel property;
interfere with safety equipment;
engage in unauthorized commercial activity;
allow unauthorized persons to stay overnight;
possess prohibited substances or materials; or
engage in conduct that creates a significant safety risk.
19.8 Maintenance and Damage

Students must report maintenance problems through the designated hostel maintenance process.

A student may be held financially responsible for damage caused intentionally or through serious negligence.

Normal wear resulting from reasonable use should not normally be charged to the resident.

20. Hostel Leave and Overnight Absence
20.1 Overnight Absence

A resident planning to remain away from the hostel overnight should follow the hostel's prescribed absence-reporting process.

The university may require residents to provide the expected date of return.

20.2 Extended Absence

Students expecting to remain away from the hostel for multiple consecutive nights should notify hostel administration according to the applicable procedure.

Extended absence does not automatically terminate hostel accommodation.

20.3 Emergency Absence

A resident who must leave unexpectedly because of an emergency should notify the hostel administration as soon as reasonably possible.

The administration may request supporting information when necessary for safety or record-keeping purposes.

21. Hostel Safety
21.1 Fire Safety

Students must comply with fire-safety instructions and evacuation procedures.

Fire exits, alarms, extinguishers, and emergency access routes must not be obstructed.

21.2 Electrical Equipment

Students must use electrical appliances responsibly and in accordance with hostel safety requirements.

The hostel administration may prohibit appliances that create an unreasonable electrical or fire risk.

21.3 Emergency Reporting

Students should immediately report serious safety incidents, hazards, or emergencies to hostel security or the designated university authority.

Students should not interfere with emergency equipment or attempt to disable safety systems.

22. Disciplinary Regulations
22.1 Scope

Disciplinary regulations apply to conduct that violates university rules, materially disrupts university activities, damages university interests, or creates a significant risk to members of the university community.

22.2 Disciplinary Offences

Examples of conduct that may result in disciplinary proceedings include:

academic misconduct;
examination misconduct;
harassment or serious intimidation;
deliberate damage to university property;
unauthorized access to restricted university systems or facilities;
submission of materially false information;
serious disruption of academic activities; and
repeated violation of university regulations.
22.3 Preliminary Review

When a possible disciplinary violation is reported, the designated authority may conduct a preliminary review.

The purpose of the preliminary review is to determine whether there is sufficient basis for formal disciplinary proceedings.

22.4 Notice to Student

A student subject to formal disciplinary proceedings should receive notice of the allegation and an opportunity to respond.

The notice should provide sufficient information for the student to understand the nature of the allegation.

22.5 Student Response

A student may submit a written response and relevant supporting material within the period specified in the disciplinary notice.

Failure to respond within the specified period may allow the university to proceed using the information available to it.

22.6 Disciplinary Committee

Where a matter requires formal committee review, the case may be referred to the designated disciplinary committee.

The committee may review relevant records, statements, documents, and other evidence.

22.7 Possible Outcomes

Depending on the nature and seriousness of the violation, disciplinary outcomes may include:

written warning;
formal reprimand;
academic or administrative penalty;
restriction from specified university activities;
suspension; or
other consequence expressly authorized by university regulations.

The outcome should be proportionate to the circumstances and applicable regulations.

23. Academic Misconduct
23.1 Plagiarism

Plagiarism includes presenting another person's work, ideas, analysis, or writing as one's own without appropriate acknowledgment.

Students are responsible for following the citation and attribution requirements applicable to their course or programme.

23.2 Unauthorized Collaboration

Students must not collaborate on an assessment when the instructor or assessment instructions require individual work.

Collaboration that is expressly permitted by the instructor is not considered unauthorized collaboration.

23.3 Fabrication

Fabrication includes knowingly creating false academic information, experimental results, records, references, or other material and presenting it as genuine.

23.4 Examination Misconduct

Examination misconduct may include copying, unauthorized communication, possession or use of prohibited materials, impersonation, or other attempts to obtain unauthorized assistance.

23.5 Penalties

Academic misconduct may result in penalties under the disciplinary process.

The applicable authority should consider the nature of the misconduct, relevant evidence, previous violations where permitted, and the applicable academic regulations.

24. Appeals Against Disciplinary Decisions
24.1 Appeal Eligibility

A student may appeal a disciplinary decision when an appeal mechanism is provided by the applicable regulation.

24.2 Grounds for Appeal

An appeal may be based on:

procedural irregularity;
relevant evidence that was not reasonably available during the original review;
a material error in applying the applicable regulation; or
another ground expressly recognized by the appeal procedure.
24.3 Appeal Deadline

An appeal must normally be submitted within the deadline stated in the disciplinary decision.

Late appeals may be considered only when the applicable authority permits an extension.

24.4 Appeal Decision

The designated appeal authority may uphold, modify, or overturn the original decision where permitted by the applicable procedure.

The appeal authority should provide or record the basis for its decision.

25. University Property
25.1 Responsible Use

Students must use classrooms, laboratories, libraries, hostel facilities, computing resources, and other university property responsibly.

25.2 Damage

A student who intentionally damages university property may be subject to disciplinary action and may be required to compensate the university where authorized.

25.3 Lost Property

Lost property found on university premises should be submitted to the designated lost-and-found or administrative office.

Students should not retain property belonging to another person.

End of Part 3

